# Copy this file to `.env.local` to point your local dev server at the
# LOCAL Supabase stack (Docker) instead of the hosted project. `.env.local`
# always overrides `.env` in Vite, and it's already gitignored (see the
# `*.local` rule in .gitignore).
#
# Why this matters: without this file, `npm run dev` falls back to the
# hosted URL/key baked into `.env`, so every sign up / sign in / page
# navigation during local testing was hitting the HOSTED project's rate
# limits — that's the actual cause of the 429 errors, not a bug in the
# login code itself. Point at local Supabase and you get generous limits
# (see supabase/config.toml) plus a local inbox for confirmation emails.
#
# Steps:
#   1. `supabase start`  (requires Docker Desktop running)
#   2. `supabase status` — copy the "API URL" and "anon key" values below
#      (defaults shown match a fresh `supabase init`, but always confirm)
#   3. Restart `npm run dev` after saving this file — Vite only reads env
#      files at server start, changes here don't hot-reload.
#   4. Confirmation emails land at http://127.0.0.1:54324 (Inbucket/Mailpit),
#      not your real inbox.

VITE_SUPABASE_URL="http://127.0.0.1:54321"
VITE_SUPABASE_PROJECT_ID="local"
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY="paste-the-anon-key-from-supabase-status-here"

# Only needed if you run server-side/admin code locally (e.g. testing
# src/integrations/supabase/client.server.ts). Never commit a real service
# role key — this is the local stack's key, which is a fixed, public,
# non-secret value from `supabase status` when running locally.
SUPABASE_URL="http://127.0.0.1:54321"
SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY="paste-the-anon-key-from-supabase-status-here"
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY="paste-the-service-role-key-from-supabase-status-here"
